## What happened

Apple's security frameworks (Security.framework / CryptoKit) **do not support DSA**. In earlier .NET versions, the runtime silently fell back to an OpenSSL-based implementation on macOS. In .NET 11, that fallback was removed — `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS.

Linux and Windows are unaffected because they use OpenSSL and Windows CNG respectively, both of which still support DSA.

## How to fix it

Since you only need to **verify** legacy signatures (not generate new ones), the pragmatic fix is to use **BouncyCastle** (a managed, cross-platform crypto library):

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

Then replace the platform-dependent `DSA.Create()` path with BouncyCastle's managed DSA verifier:

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Signers;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(
    byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] message, byte[] signature)
{
    var publicKey = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)
        PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);

    var signer = new DsaSigner();
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);

    // Hash the message the same way your legacy code did (typically SHA-1)
    var digest = DigestUtilities.GetDigest("SHA-1");
    digest.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    var hash = new byte[digest.GetDigestSize()];
    digest.DoFinal(hash, 0);

    // Decode the DER-encoded signature into (r, s)
    var derSeq = (Org.BouncyCastle.Asn1.Asn1Sequence)
        Org.BouncyCastle.Asn1.Asn1Object.FromByteArray(signature);
    var r = ((Org.BouncyCastle.Asn1.DerInteger)derSeq[0]).Value;
    var s = ((Org.BouncyCastle.Asn1.DerInteger)derSeq[1]).Value;

    return signer.VerifySignature(hash, r, s);
}
```

### Going forward

- **Migrate new signatures** to ECDSA or Ed25519 — both are cross-platform in .NET.
- Keep BouncyCastle only for verifying the legacy DSA backlog; remove it once all legacy signatures are retired.
